---
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const asset = (path: string) => `${base}${path}`;
const link = (path: string) => `${base}${path}`;
---
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="description" content="Get started with later, durable time and workflows for Ruby." />
    <link rel="icon" href={asset('favicon.svg')} />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href={asset('styles/site.css')} />
    <title>Getting started — later</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="site-shell site-nav">
      <a href={link('')} aria-label="later home"><img src={asset('brand/later-wordmark.svg')} alt="later" /></a>
      <nav class="site-nav-links" aria-label="Primary navigation">
        <a href={link('')}>Home</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/theworker02/later">GitHub</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main class="site-shell docs-main">
      <div class="eyebrow">Getting started</div>
      <h1>Make time durable.</h1>
      <p class="hero-lede">`later` is a local-first temporal runtime for plain Ruby. It persists work in SQLite, recovers leased jobs after failures, and gives you a small CLI for operations.</p>

      <div class="notice"><strong>Prerelease boundary:</strong> this development line targets Ruby 3.2+, local SQLite, and at-least-once delivery. PostgreSQL, Rails/Active Job, hosted dashboards, network transports, full calendar/DST parsing, and OpenTelemetry exporters are not shipped core features.</div>

      <h2>1. Install</h2>
      <p>Install the gem into an application with a working SQLite native extension:</p>
      <pre><code>gem install later</code></pre>
      <p>For Bundler:</p>
      <pre><code>gem "later"</code></pre>

      <h2>2. Configure a database</h2>
      <p>Point the runtime at an application-owned SQLite path before scheduling work:</p>
      <pre><code>require "later"

Later.configure(path: "tmp/later.sqlite3")</code></pre>

      <h2>3. Schedule durable work</h2>
      <p>Use named dispatch for work that must survive a process restart:</p>
      <pre><code>class Reports
  def self.generate_monthly(month:)
    # Build the report.
  end
end

Later.call(Reports, :generate_monthly,
  at: "2026-09-01 09:00",
  month: "2026-08")</code></pre>
      <p>Use intervals or weekday rules for recurring work:</p>
      <pre><code>Later.every("15m") &#123; Metrics.flush &#125;
Later.every("weekday at 09:00", timezone: "UTC") &#123; Digest.send &#125;</code></pre>

      <h2>4. Run and inspect the worker</h2>
      <pre><code>later run --db tmp/later.sqlite3
later jobs --json --db tmp/later.sqlite3
later doctor --db tmp/later.sqlite3
later db check --db tmp/later.sqlite3</code></pre>

      <h2>5. Explore streams and workflows</h2>
      <p>Use <code>Later.workflow</code> for validated dependency graphs and <code>Later::Stream</code> for durable append-only events with consumer groups. These surfaces are part of the prerelease line and retain the same at-least-once delivery model.</p>
      <p>Continue with the repository documentation for <a href="https://github.com/theworker02/later/blob/main/docs/streams.md">streams</a>, <a href="https://github.com/theworker02/later/blob/main/docs/scheduling/timezones.md">time zones</a>, <a href="https://github.com/theworker02/later/blob/main/docs/security/serialization.md">serialization safety</a>, and the <a href="https://github.com/theworker02/later/blob/main/ROADMAP.md">roadmap</a>.</p>

      <h2>Reliability model</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Jobs are persisted before dispatch and claimed through leases.</li>
        <li>Expired leases make eligible work available for recovery.</li>
        <li>External effects are at-least-once, not exactly-once.</li>
        <li>Handlers and consumers should be repeat-safe and use idempotency keys.</li>
      </ul>
    </main>

    <footer class="site-shell site-footer">
      <p><strong>later</strong> — Durable time and workflows for Ruby.</p>
      <p><a href={link('')}>Back to home</a> · <a href="https://github.com/theworker02/later">Source on GitHub</a></p>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
